{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law","requirement":"A landlord may not take a security deposit above one and one-half times one month's rent, must hold it in trust and pay the tenant the interest, and must return it with itemized deductions within 30 days after the tenancy ends.","key_value":"1.5 months' rent; annual deposit increase cap 10%","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; prepaid rent, including last month's rent, counts toward the deposit cap.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Seasonal use or rental of 125 consecutive days or fewer excluded"},"exemptions":"Security deposits for seasonal use or rental, meaning a term of not more than 125 consecutive days for residential purposes by a person whose permanent residence is elsewhere.","penalty":"The tenant may sue for double the amount wrongfully withheld, plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2","quoted_span":"The maximum-security deposit to be collected by the landlord cannot be more than one and one-half times one month’s rent ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2 ). It can be less. Any additional yearly security deposit increase may not exceed 10% of the current security deposit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Safe Housing Act return of security deposit after domestic-violence termination","requirement":"When a tenant ends a lease under the Safe Housing Act because of domestic violence, the landlord must make the deposit plus interest available within 15 business days and notify the tenant in writing within three business days where to collect it.","key_value":"15 business days","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who terminate a residential lease under the Safe Housing Act because the tenant or a child faces an imminent threat of serious physical harm from a specific person.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Transient or seasonal rentals excluded"},"exemptions":"Transient or seasonal rentals.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6","quoted_span":"Within 15 business days after the lease is terminated, the landlord shall make available the return of the tenant’s security deposit, plus any interest earned, to the tenant or the tenant’s agent.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
